  "oom_vs_external_kill_analysis": {
    "paragraph": "OBSERVED OOM and UNKNOWN_EXTERNAL_KILL are different epistemic categories and the controller keeps them separate by construction. An OOM label is only licensed when a death is abnormal (SIGKILL the controller itself never sent) AND contemporaneous memory evidence actually exists: the kernel's memorystatus pressure level at/above CRITICAL (4), an allocator-level failure captured in the worker's own stderr tail (e.g. MemoryError / jetsam report), or an explicit memory-status kill record. Absent that evidence the same death is recorded as UNKNOWN_EXTERNAL_KILL with memory_pressure_evidence=null -- 'some external actor or the kernel killed it, reason unproven' -- because inferring OOM from a SIGKILL alone would launder an assumption into a measurement. In this sprint no probe worker died abnormally at all: all six probe workers across three two-worker rounds exited 0 and verified COMPLETED, so there is nothing to classify; nonetheless the macOS pressure level stayed at WARN(2) while the pair concurrently addressed 2560 MiB (kernel compressed anonymous pages rather than escalating to critical or killing anything). The distinction therefore remains live and enforced: any future SIGKILL-without-evidence lands in UNKNOWN_EXTERNAL_KILL, never in OOM.",
